  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who are white.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people who are descended from Arab peoples and were born in the United States, or LGBTQ+ people born in Arab countries who live in or have a strong connection to the United States. Do not conflate with Muslim people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Lesbians who are descended from Arab peoples and were born in the United States, or lesbians born in Arab countries who live in or have a strong connection to the United States. Do not conflate with Muslim people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gay men who are descended from Arab peoples and were born in the United States, or gay men born in Arab countries who live in or have a strong connection to the United States. Do not conflate with Muslim people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Bisexual people who are descended from Arab peoples and were born in the United States, or bisexual people born in Arab countries who live in or have a strong connection to the United States. Do not conflate with Muslim people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Transgender people who are descended from Arab peoples and were born in the United States, or transgender people born in Arab countries who live in or have a strong connection to the United States. Do not conflate with Muslim people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Queer people who are descended from Arab peoples and were born in the United States, or Queer people born in Arab countries who live in or have a strong connection to the United States. Do not conflate with Muslim people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who are descended from Arab peoples and were born in the United States, or non-binary people born in Arab countries who live in or have a strong connection to the United States. Do not conflate with Muslim people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Asexual people who are descended from Arab peoples and were born in the United States, or asexual people born in Arab countries who live in or have a strong connection to the United States. Do not conflate with Muslim people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Intersex people who are descended from Arab peoples and were born in the United States, or intersex people born in Arab countries who live in or have a strong connection to the United States. Do not conflate with Muslim people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Aromantic people who are descended from Arab peoples and were born in the United States, or aromantic people born in Arab countries who live in or have a strong connection to the United States. Do not conflate with Muslim people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Aromantic people who are from Arab countries and/or are descended from Arab peoples. Do not conflate with Muslim people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who belong to one or more of the various Indigenous peoples of Australia; do not use to refer to the Indigenous people of the Torres Strait Islands, who are considered ethnically and culturally distinct. Only use to describe people who identify as Aboriginal Australian.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who are a part of the socio-ethnic Assamese peoples, speak the Assamese language, or reside in the Indian state of Assam.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who speak the Awadhi dialect and reside in or are descended from those who resided in the Awadh region of Uttar Pradesh.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who speak the Bengali language or are of Bengali descent. </subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who speak the Bhil language and belong to or are descended from the Bhil tribal group.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who speak the Bhojpuri language and live in or are descended from those who inhabited the Bhojpuri-Purvanchal region of India and Nepal.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who belong to two or more racial groups. Only use to describe people who self-identify as biracial.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Black people who are non-binary. Use only for individuals who self-identity as Black. Follow the individual&#x2019;s preference for African American or Black whenever possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who belong to or are descended from the Chakma tribal group.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people of Mexican descent born in the United States; only use to describe people who self-identify as Chicano, Chicana, or Chicanx. Do not conflate with Latino/a/x or Hispanic people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who do not identify as such to others.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Daughters who have one or more non-binary parent.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who are of the Dogra people of India and Pakistan and speak the Dogri language.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">People who assume the role of father to non-binary people. Only use to describe individuals who identify as fathers.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who belong to or are descended from those native to Garhwal in Uttarakhand and who speak Garhwali.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who reside in or are descended from those indigenous to the Indian state of Gujarat.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who are indigenous to or descended from those indigenous to what is now the Indian state of Haryana. This term can also apply to those who currently reside in Haryana.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who are Inuit, or part of several Indigenous peoples inhabiting the Inuit homeland in the Arctic and subarctic regions of Greenland, Canada, and Alaska. Only use for people who identify as Inuit.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who are Jewish.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who belong to the indigenous Kalasha people and reside in the Chitral District of Khyber-Pakhtunkhwa province in Pakistan.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who are part of the Indigenous people of Mokupuni o Hawai&#x2018;i, or the Hawaiian Islands; only use to refer to people who identify as Kanaka Maoli.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who live in or are descended from those native to the Kashmir Valley in the Indian subcontinent.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who belong to or are descended from the people indigenous to the Chitral and Ghizer districts of modern-day Pakistan.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who are from or descended from those native to the Konkan region of the Indian subcontinent, and who speak any of the dialects of the Konkani language.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who live mostly in Kumaon region in the Indian Himalayas. They might also speak the Kumaoni language as their first language.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who are from or are descended from those native to the Kutch district of the Indian state of Gujarat and the Sindh region of Pakistan.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Latin American people who are non-binary; only use to describe individuals who self-identify as Latino, Latina, or Latinx. Do not conflate with Chicano/a/x or Hispanic.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who belong to or are descended from the people native to the Maldives (including the island of Minicoy) and speak the Maldivian language.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who belong to two or more racial groups.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">People who are part of the Native peoples of the continental United States and Canada (excluding Inuit people) and self-identity as Non-binary. Do not conflate with Two-Spirit people. Use more specific terms when available.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who identify as both Pacific Islanders (or of Pacific Islander ancestry) and American.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who are Papuan, or part of the Indigenous peoples of new Guinea; only use for people who identify as Papuan.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who are Romani, or part of the traditionally nomadic Indo-European people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who belong to or are descended from the ethnolinguistic groups of South Asia.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Works consisting of sequential images often accompanied by text that generally contain stand-alone stories; used in reference to graphic novels focusing on gay male characters or themes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Works consisting of sequential images often accompanied by text that generally contain stand-alone stories; used in reference to graphic novels focusing on lesbian characters or themes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Works consisting of sequential images often accompanied by text that generally contain stand-alone stories. Use in reference to graphic novels focusing on bisexual characters or themes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Works consisting of sequential images often accompanied by text that generally contain stand-alone stories; used in reference to graphic novels focusing on megasexual characters or themes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Works consisting of sequential images often accompanied by text that generally contain stand-alone stories; used in reference to graphic novels focusing on transgender characters or themes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Works consisting of sequential images often accompanied by text that generally contain stand-alone stories; used in reference to graphic novels focusing on queer characters or themes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Images and videos that show children and youth who are under the age of consent engaged in sexual activities. While this term is not otherwise within the scope of the Homosaurus vocabulary, it is provided as an alternative to commonly used terms such as "Child pornography" in order to highlight that the production of such materials is a direct product of and contributes to the sexual abuse of children.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Queer people who paint, either professionally or as a hobby.</subfield>
  </datafield>
