  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Efforts to propose, enact, amend, or strike down laws as a means of restricting the legal rights of transgender people. This includes legislation regarding access to gender-affirming healthcare, participation in sporting events, administrative and judicial procedures such as sex and name registration, and policies affecting the medical treatment, education, and mental health care of transgender youth.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A genre focusing on the romantic or sexual relationships between male characters in Chinese novels, comics, movies, animation, and dramas.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Babies conceived through at-home artificial insemination, specifically by injecting semen into one's vagina using a turkey baster. There is an extensive history of at-home artificial insemination undertaken by LGBTQ+ people, including gay men contributing semen to aid lesbian couples in conceiving.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Laws or policies instituted that prevent trans and non-binary youth and children from seeking and/or obtaining medical treatment or gender-affirming care, including but not limited to surgery, hormone treatment, and mental health care. </subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Laws or policies instituted to prevent trans and non-binary people from changing the gender marker on their birth certificate, driver's license, passport, or other legal documents.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Laws or policies instituted to prevent trans and non-binary individuals from seeking medical treatment or gender-affirming care, including but not limited to surgery, hormone treatment, and mental health care. </subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A slang term to describe a gathering of friends for the purpose of chatting and gossiping. The term evolved out of the 1990s ballroom scene in New York City and was further popularized by the 2012 song "Let's Have a Kiki" by the Scissor Sisters. Do not conflate with the term "Ky-ky (Gender role)," sometimes also spelled "Kiki," which refers to lesbians who do not identify as either butches or femmes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A slang term that emerged in the 1940s to describe lesbians who do not identify as butch or femme. Do not conflate with the term "Kiki (Social gathering), which refers to a gathering of friends.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A genre focusing on the romantic or sexual relationships between female characters in anime, dramas, comics, novels, and movies. The term is usually used to describe media originating from Asia.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A genre focusing on the romantic or sexual relationships between female characters in Chinese novels, comics, movies, animation, and dramas. </subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A term used to describe individuals who are in the process of determining their sexual orientation and/or gender identity. </subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Laws or policies instituted to prevent trans and non-binary individuals from participating in gendered sports leagues or competitions, typically to force players to compete as their sex assigned at birth rather than as their gender. This ranges from criminalizing schools and programs that allow trans and non-binary youth participation to banning players, particularly trans women, from competing in gendered leagues or competitions.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people who paint, either professionally or as a hobby.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Efforts to change the gender identity of a transgender or non-binary person to meet cisgender norms, typically under the belief that their identity is dangerous and/or the result of mental illness or trauma. The processes involved in conversion therapy include visualization training, spiritual interventions, and other aversion treatments. </subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gay men who paint, either professionally or as a hobby.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Organized measures taken for the maintenance or improvement of health via the prevention, diagnosis, and/or treatment of physical and mental health issues faced by transgender children and youth. This includes but is not limited to access to safe, legal therapy and counseling, hormone treatment, and gender-affirming surgery.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The study of language as used by members of LGBTQ+ communities.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Lesbians who paint, either professionally or as a hobby.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Bisexual people who paint, either professionally or as a hobby.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Trans people who paint, either professionally or as a hobby.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A cultural system in which positions of dominance and privilege are held by men, while women and other gender minorities are largely excluded from access to power.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A field of linguistics that analyzes the effect of heteronormativity on the expression of sexual identity through language.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Organized groups centered on political change and action for LGBTQ+ rights that may or may not be aligned with major political parties.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Organized groups centered on political change and action for bisexual people's rights.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Organized groups centered on political change and action for queer people's rights; may include advocacy and other political efforts for LGBTQ+ people's rights more broadly.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Organized groups centered on political change and action for megasexual people's rights.</subfield>
  </datafield>
