  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Collections of records and materials relating to Black queer people and communities, or collections of records and materials relating to Black people and communities that are curated, organized, and presented from a queer perspective. Can include a variety of organizations and institutions from community to government archives.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Religious services, or gatherings of people belonging to the same religious group or belief system, that are accepting and supportive of LGBTQ+ people and their identities. Includes services specifically intended for LGBTQ+ people within a denomination or sect that is otherwise not LGBTQ+-affirming.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Music created by LGBTQ+ artists, for LGBTQ+ audiences, and/or that expresses an LGBTQ+ sensibility.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Rap music created by LGBTQ+ artists, for LGBTQ+ audiences, and/or that expresses an LGBTQ+ sensibility.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Pop music created by LGBTQ+ artists, for LGBTQ+ audiences, and/or that expresses an LGBTQ+ sensibility.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Term used to describe the ways in which women experience and express themselves sexually, including the types of people and practices they are sexually interested in.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A school of architecture that seeks to create spaces that critique normative conceptions of gender and sexuality.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A school of ecology that seeks to understand nature, biology, and sexuality through the framing of queer theory. Queer ecology rejects the presumption of heterosexuality and cisgenderedness as natural, as well as notions of human exceptionalism and anthropocentrism.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A school of thought that rejects Eurocentric knowledge hierarchies and ways of being, including the presumed superiority of white Western culture.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people whose profession involves managing information by collecting, organizing, storing and disseminating it. This can include but is not limited to librarians, archivists, metadata and IT workers, web developers, and chief information officers. Use more specific terms when possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people, typically with degrees in library science, who work, manage, or assist in a library setting.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Two-Spirit people whose profession involves managing information by collecting, organizing, storing and disseminating it. This can include but is not limited to librarians, archivists, metadata and IT workers, web developers, and chief information officers. Use more specific terms when possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Lesbians, typically with degrees in library science, who work, manage, or assist in a library setting. </subfield>
