  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A process of economic and demographic change within a historically disinvested neighborhood by means of real estate investment and an influx of new, higher-income residents, typically resulting in the displacement of long-time, lower-income residents.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A discriminatory practice in which financial services are withheld from neighborhoods that have significant numbers of racial and ethnic minorities. Redlining includes but is not limited to denial of credit, insurance, and healthcare; inflated mortgage and interest rates; and the development of food deserts. The term has its roots in the New Deal when color-coded maps were made of metropolitan areas in the United States to indicate where it was considered safe to insure mortgages. Black neighborhoods and surrounding areas were colored red to indicate mortgages should not be insured there.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Public spaces designated as safe for, or accepting and affirming of, LGBTQ+ people, often by allies. LGBTQ+ safe spaces are sometimes identified through the display of stickers or placards.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The particular impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on LGBTQ+ people and communities, including changes in access to health care, intimate relationship dynamics and safer sex practices, financial stability, social connectedness, and mental health.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Efforts to improve the welfare of LGBTQ+ people and communities, especially by the donation of money or resources to charitable causes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people who seek to improve the welfare of others, especially by the donation of money or resources to charitable causes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people involved in the punk community, which originated in the 1970s with punk rock music and is now associated with various music subgenres, fashions, and ideologies.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A subset of the punk community to which LGBTQ+ punks and allies belong. The punk community originated in the 1970s with punk rock music and is now associated with various music subgrenes, fashions, and ideologies.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Spaces maintained and intended for use by LGBTQ+ people. Includes physical spaces as well as online spaces.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The analysis and evaluation of LGBTQ+ films and film history, or film criticism from an LGBTQ+ perspective.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The analysis and evaluation of lesbian films and film history, or film criticism from a lesbian perspective.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The analysis and evaluation of gay films and film history, or film criticism from a gay perspective.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The analysis and evaluation of bisexual films and film history, or film criticism from a bisexual perspective.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The analysis and evaluation of trans films and film history, or film criticism from a trans perspective.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The analysis and evaluation of queer films and film history, or film criticism from a queer perspective.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The analysis and evaluation of feminist films and film history, or film criticism from a feminist perspective.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The intersection of decolonial and queer theory, which critiques Eurocentric conceptions of gender and sexuality as a tool of colonization.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A positive feeling connected to trans identity, being, community, activism, and/or culture that counters the impact of living in a discriminatory society. </subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The act of engaging one's or one's partner(s) senses in various ways to heighten erotic pleasure.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Organized presentations of films in a single location, often with prizes awarded in various categories. Use in reference to film festivals focusing on films created by lesbians or featuring lesbian characters and themes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Organized presentations of films in a single location, often with prizes awarded in various categories. Use in reference to film festivals focusing on films created by gay men or featuring gay characters and themes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Organized presentations of films in a single location, often with prizes awarded in various categories. Use in reference to film festivals focusing on films created by bisexual people or featuring bisexual characters and themes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Organized presentations of films in a single location, often with prizes awarded in various categories. Use in reference to film festivals focusing on films created by trans people or featuring trans characters and themes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Organized presentations of films in a single location, often with prizes awarded in various categories. Use in reference to film festivals focusing on films created by queer people or featuring queer characters and themes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Organized presentations of films in a single location, often with prizes awarded in various categories. Use in reference to film festivals focusing on films created by non-binary people or featuring non-binary characters and themes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Organized presentations of films in a single location, often with prizes awarded in various categories. Use in reference to film festivals focusing on films created by intersex people or featuring intersex characters and themes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Organized presentations of films in a single location, often with prizes awarded in various categories. Use in reference to film festivals focusing on films created by Two-Spirit people or featuring Two-Spirit characters and themes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Drinking establishments that primarily cater to gay men who identify as bears, or hairy and large or muscular.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Drinking establishments that primarily cater to dykes. For establishments that cater to lesbians more broadly, use "Lesbian bars."</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-fictional films that center LGBTQ+ people or topics, created for the purposes of education, advocacy, or personal expression.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-fictional films that center lesbians or lesbian topics, created for the purposes of education, advocacy, or personal expression.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-fictional films that center LGBTQ+ gay men or topics, created for the purposes of education, advocacy, or personal expression.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-fictional films that center bisexual people or topics, created for the purposes of education, advocacy, or personal expression.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-fictional films that center trans people or topics, created for the purposes of education, advocacy, or personal expression.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-fictional films that center queer people or topics, created for the purposes of education, advocacy, or personal expression.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-fictional films that center non-binary people or topics, created for the purposes of education, advocacy, or personal expression.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-fictional films that center intersex people or topics, created for the purposes of education, advocacy, or personal expression.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-fictional films that center Two-Spirit people or topics, created for the purposes of education, advocacy, or personal expression.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-fictional films that center asexual people or topics, created for the purposes of education, advocacy, or personal expression.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-fictional films that center aromantic people or topics, created for the purposes of education, advocacy, or personal expression.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-fictional films that center demisexual people or topics, created for the purposes of education, advocacy, or personal expression.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-fictional films that center greyromantic people or topics, created for the purposes of education, advocacy, or personal expression.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-fictional films that center megasexual people or topics, created for the purposes of education, advocacy, or personal expression.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Festivals held on public streets that celebrate the character of the surrounding neighborhood or community. Use to refer to street fairs specifically celebrating the local LGBTQ+ community.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Large formal meetings or assemblies of LGBTQ+ people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Twisting or squeezing the clitoris to induce sexual pleasure.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A movement that opposes what is referred to as "gender ideology," or the belief that gender is a social construct that exists on a spectrum. Members of the anti-gender movement oppose the visibility, acceptance, and civil and reproductive rights of trans, non-binary, intersex, Two-Spirit, gender diverse, and gender non-conforming people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The idea that a person's sexual orientation can change over time or in different situations.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">People who are attracted to all genders except women, femmes, and feminine-aligned non-binary people, regardless of what gender they were assigned at birth.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">People who are attracted to all genders except men, mascs, and masculine-aligned non-binary people, regardless of what gender they were assigned at birth. </subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">People who are solely attracted to androgynous non-binary people, rather than monogender-aligned people, regardless of what gender they were assigned at birth.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The trade and exploitation of human beings through force, fraud, and/or coercion, often with the intention of compelling the individual to provide sexual services or other forms of labor against their will.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Human trafficking for the purpose of sexual exploitation, including recruiting, holding, moving, defrauding, and/or coercing people into engaging in sexual services and performances they cannot or have not meaningfully consented to.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Human trafficking for the purpose of labor exploitation, including recruiting, holding, moving, defrauding, and/or coercing people into labor, such as through debt bondage or involuntary servitude, against their will.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people who have experienced and may or may not have survived human trafficking. Follow the individual's preference for "victims" or "survivors" whenever possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people who have experienced and survived human trafficking. Follow the individual's preference for "victims" or "survivors" whenever possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Two-Spirit people who have experienced and may or may not have survived human trafficking. Follow the individual's preference for "victims" or "survivors" whenever possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Two-Spirit people who have experienced crimes and may or may not have survived. Use more specific terms and follow the individual's preference for "victims" or "survivors" whenever possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Two-Spirit people who have experienced and survived human trafficking. Follow the individual's preference for "victims" or "survivors" whenever possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people who have experienced and may or may not have survived sex trafficking. Follow the individual's preference for "victims" or "survivors" whenever possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people who have experienced and survived sex trafficking. Follow the individual's preference for "victims" or "survivors" whenever possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Two-Spirit people who have experienced and may or may not have survived sex trafficking. Follow the individual's preference for "victims" or "survivors" whenever possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Two-Spirit people who have experienced and survived sex trafficking. Follow the individual's preference for "victims" or "survivors" whenever possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people who have experienced and may or may not have survived labor trafficking. Follow the individual's preference for "victims" or "survivors" whenever possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people who have experienced and survived labor trafficking. Follow the individual's preference for "victims" or "survivors" whenever possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Two-Spirit people who have experienced and may or may not have survived labor trafficking. Follow the individual's preference for "victims" or "survivors" whenever possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Two-Spirit people who have experienced and survived labor trafficking. Follow the individual's preference for "victims" or "survivors" whenever possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">People who receive money or goods in exchange for sexual services, performances, or companionship. Use to describe people working across the broad spectrum of sex work, and use more specific terms if possible</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Intersex people who receive money or goods in exchange for sexual services, performances, or companionship. Use to describe intersex people working across the broad spectrum of sex work.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary people who receive money or goods in exchange for sexual services, performances, or companionship. Use to describe non-binary people working across the broad spectrum of sex work.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Two-Spirit people who receive money or goods in exchange for sexual services, performances, or companionship. Use to describe Two-Spirit people working across the broad spectrum of sex work.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">M&#x101;h&#x16B; who receive money or goods in exchange for sexual services, performances, or companionship. Use to describe M&#x101;h&#x16B; working across the broad spectrum of sex work.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Real-time sex work via phone calls, text messaging, or online voice calls. Use "Cam modeling" for real-time sex work via online video calls and livestreams.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A form of sex work that involves undressing to music while using a pole to perform dance moves, whether in a venue like a strip club or by hire at private parties. Stripping may also be performed by amateurs in private settings.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A form of feminism that alleges that sex work is inherently coercive and exploitative. Although not all sex workers are women and not all clients are men, anti-sex work feminism largely centers this gender dynamic, viewing sex work as a result and reinforcement of the patriarchy.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">An acronym for "sex work-exclusionary radical feminists," this term refers to people who subscribe to a subset of radical feminism that rejects sex worker rights and solidarity with sex workers under the belief that sex work is antithetical to their ideology and inherently perpetuates oppression against women.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A form of feminism that alleges that pornography is a result and reinforcement of the patriarchy and is thus inherently violent and exploitative.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Short for "webcam modeling," this term refers to real-time sex work via online video calls and livestreams.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Short for "webcam models," this term refers to sex workers who exchange sexual services, performances, or companionship via online video calls and livestreams. </subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Short for "webcam boys," this term refers to men sex workers who exchange sexual services, performances, or companionship via online video calls and livestreams. Use only for individuals who self-identify as camboys; otherwise, use "Cam models."</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Short for "webcam girls," this term refers to women sex workers who exchange sexual services, performances, or companionship via online video calls and livestreams. Use only for individuals who self-identify as camgirls; otherwise, use "Cam models."</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Laws and regulations addressing the practice, solitiation, and/or facilitation of sex work, including its legality. Legislation may variously address the actions of sex workers, their clients, third parties, and/or other individuals who work in proximity to or seek to aid sex workers.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The process by which the practice and solicitation of sex work is made criminal, typically by introducing laws, regulations, and criminal penalties. Efforts to criminalize sex work often target street sex work and other forms of "direct," visible sex work.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The process of lessening or removing laws, regulations, and/or criminal penalties targeting the practice, solicitation, and/or facilitation of sex work. Decriminalization efforts may variously address the actions of sex workers, their clients, third parties, and/or other individuals who work in proximity to or seek to aid sex workers.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The creation of a set of laws and regulations intended to legalize the practice and solicitation of sex work, particularly street sex work and other forms of "direct," visible sex work, within certain parameters. This may variously include legalizing the actions of sex workers, their clients, third parties, and/or other individuals who work in proximity to or seek to aid sex workers. Individuals operating outside of these laws and regulations remain subject to penalties.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A legislative approach to sex work first instituted in Sweden in 1999 and then in Norway in 2009 that centers on the criminalization of clients, third parties, and many of the ways sex workers operate. The Nordic model is a form of sex work abolition predicated on the idea that no person can consent to engage in commercial sexual services and that sex workers must therefore be educated and reformed. Sex workers and activists have heavily criticized and opposed the Nordic model.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A movement to abolish sex work through a variety of different means, including supporting "end demand" laws, the Nordic model, or the broader criminalization of sex work; organizing and speaking vocally against sex work as inherently exploitative; and rejecting efforts to recognize sex work as labor.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The practice of driving or idling in one's car around areas known for street sex work in order to solicit sex workers.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The act of offering or attempting to exchange money or goods for sexual services or performances.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Sex work that is generally solicited in a public place such as a street, street corner, or park.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A form of sex work performed in lieu of other employment or in order to meet immediate needs, such as shelter, food, clothing, familial care, etc. Use only for sex work that is self-described as survival.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A legal term for individuals who supervise, coordinate, assist, or facilitate sex work, either on behalf of sex workers or their clients. Third parties include managers, brothel-keepers, concierges, drivers etc.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">People who mediate between sex workers and clients. This term has historically been used to describe women but can be used for individuals of any gender based on their preference. Follow the individual's preference for "Madams," "Pimps," or "Sex work concierges" whenever possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">People who mediate between sex workers and clients. Since the 20th century, this term has been used as a derogatory way of describing urban, working-class Black American men, regardless of their involvement in the facilitation of sex work. While some Black Americans--including women--have embraced an evolved version of the word "pimp," often stripped of its association with sex work facilitation, it still carries racist and classist connotations and should be used with caution. Follow the individual's preference for "Pimps," "Madams," or "Sex work concierges" whenever possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">People who mediate between sex workers and clients. Follow the individual's preference for "Sex work concierges," "Pimps," or "Madams" whenever possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The unjust or prejudicial treatment of sex workers due to their work or identity as a sex worker; may include the denial of legal rights, employment, and housing.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Behavior involving physical force intended to harm or kill sex workers on the basis of their work or identity as a sex worker.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Intentional killing of sex workers on the basis of their work or identity as a sex worker.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A set of practical strategies and ideas aimed at reducing the negative impacts of sex work on sex workers, as well as a social justice movement advocating for the rights of sex workers.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Legal, social, or ethical principles of freedom or entitlement held by or pursued by sex workers. This includes, but is not limited to, human rights, health rights, freedom from discrimination and prejudice, and labor rights.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Direct action and organizing to achieve political or social change and acceptance for sex workers.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A form of sex work in which companionship and sexual services are exchanged for money, gifts, and other material benefits. Generally but not always sugaring describes a relationship between an older client and a younger sex worker.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">People who provide companionship and sexual services to sugar mamas or daddies in exchange for money, gifts, and other material benefits.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">People who provide money, gifts, and other material benefits to sugar babies in exchange for companionship and sexual services. Follow the individual's preference for "Sugar daddies" or "Sugar mamas" whenever possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">People who provide money, gifts, and other material benefits to sugar babies in exchange for companionship and sexual services. Follow the individual's preference for "Sugar mamas" or "Sugar daddies" whenever possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Massage businesses that provide sexual services, either legally or under the table.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ sex workers who undress to music while using a pole to perform dance moves, whether in a venue like a strip club or by hire at private parties. For stripping performed by amateurs in private settings, use "Stripping."</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ actors in erotic or sexually explicit films.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Areas designated (often but not always legally) for the practice and solicitation of sex work.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Partitioned car garages found in a few countries in Europe that are designed to allow and facilitate sex work in cars. Sex drive-ins are often created by local authorities to help regulate where sex work occurs.</subfield>
